I've written two task files and an issue-responses file in session_plan/. The commit step did nothing because session_plan/ is in .gitignore. The plan's || true covers that, and the files are on disk for the next phase to read.

Task 1 (product): send the default openai model's requests through OpenAI's Responses API. yoyo's v0.3.0 default openai model is gpt-6-sol. It sends tool calls over the Chat Completions endpoint without setting reasoning_effort, and OpenAI's docs say tool calling there only works at effort none. That makes it a broken default. The code kept the old endpoint because the change "cannot be verified without a key", but the vendor docs settle that, and a stub server can check what yoyo sends without a key. The task:
• switches only the GPT-6 ids yoyo has presets for; every other openai id keeps today's behaviour exactly;
• rewrites the existing test that pinned the old behaviour (renamed so its name is true, not deleted) and keeps a check that a non-GPT-6 id is unchanged;
• adds a stub-server test of the request path and the tools field, which has to pass without API keys, as CI has none;
• fixes any doc sentence the change makes false;
• files a new issue for what's left before pushing: other openai reasoning models still go to Chat Completions, and third-party servers without a /responses endpoint will now fail for GPT-6 ids.

It will not have been tested against real OpenAI, because there's no key here.

Task 2 (product, backlog drain): close out #988. I skipped the two older backlog issues. On #902, what's left is a security design decision the issue itself says is blocked. On #944, one remaining part is in a protected workflow file and the other is a larger design. During planning I answered one of #988's three questions myself and posted it on the issue: yoyo never registers an on_error callback, so nothing could have stopped firing. Another was already fixed. The task probes the last one on the built binary: whether Ctrl-C, both while waiting on the model and while a tool runs, still prints the interrupt note and gets you back to a working prompt. If both cases pass, it adds a test for that behaviour and closes #988. If either fails, it fixes it or files a new issue.

